<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(OAMa's growing database augments studies on the natural history and ecology of local species and populations. The data is also shared and analyzed by students and partner collaborators from different research institutions, such as S\343o Paulo University, Rio Grande do Sul Federal University, Rio de Janeiro Federal University, and Santa Catarina Federal University. Below, we bring data and information from two of these collaborative studies.)/E(OAMa's growing database augments studies on the natural history and ecology of local species and populations. The data is also shared and analyzed by students and partner collaborators from different research institutions, such as S\343o Paulo University, Rio Grande do Sul Federal University, Rio de Janeiro Federal University, and Santa Catarina Federal University. Below, we bring data and information from two of these collaborative studies.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

263 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(At RPPN "Fazenda Alto da Boa Vista", Nat\341lia Inacio-Almeida carries out a research project focused on hummingbirds from the Atlantic Forest. Nat\341lia is a former OAMa trainee and a master's student at S\343o Paulo University, in the Interactions between Vertebrates and Flowers Laboratory \(LIVEFLOW\). Nat\341lia's objective is to understand the population dynamics and proportion of males and females of two hummingbirds species \(Thalurania glaucopis and Phaethornis eurynome\). The data will be essential to understand the reproductive dynamics and identify possible threats to the survival of these species. Data collection will continue until June 2025.)/E(At RPPN "Fazenda Alto da Boa Vista", Nat\341lia Inacio-Almeida carries out a research project focused on hummingbirds from the Atlantic Forest. Nat\341lia is a former OAMa trainee and a master's student at S\343o Paulo University, in the Interactions between Vertebrates and Flowers Laboratory \(LIVEFLOW\). Nat\341lia's objective is to understand the population dynamics and proportion of males and females of two hummingbirds species \(Thalurania glaucopis and Phaethornis eurynome\). The data will be essential to understand the reproductive dynamics and identify possible threats to the survival of these species. Data collection will continue until June 2025.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

